      "writtenOn": "2025-01-21T11:03:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Both salt/instance-hash are part of hidden input in DICe, so changing those change Microdroid CDIs.\n\nSo why did the salt use to be generated by pvmfw (using **true** RNG) and had to be stored and retrieved on every boot but the instance ID - used in the exact same way in DICE - can be generated by the host and could change on every boot, according to what you seem to be saying?\n\nSorry but I\u0027m just confused by how we seem to have just giving control to the host over something that had to be protected from it?\n\n\u003e Microdroid continues to need the `new_instance` flag to prevent the mentioned attack\n\nSo you agree that an unexpected `instance-id` (akin to reprovisioning the pvmfw stage) should be detected and, to do so, the first-seen instance hash has to be stored?\n\nIf not, how does pvmfw prevent this attack if if either ignores instance hash mismatches (current behavior) or doesn\u0027t even store it?\n\nOTOH, why does the attack matter if the host controls the salt anyway?",
